WoW64 dispatcher that is the same in every process, run from the AOT image (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0010)

Port of patches/fex/0006 to Madeira's FEX fork. iOS without JIT can only
run code signed into the app, so FEX's dispatcher, generated at startup, has
to be built in advance like the AOT caches.

- The dispatcher's absolute addresses move out of its code: the context and
  the addresses of SleepThread, CompileBlock and CompileSingleStep (four
  literals), Madeira's IosCbEntryLog capture buffer (now loaded from the
  frame given in x0; an entry with a null frame is not captured) and the
  pause handler's jump back to the FillSRA loop top (now a PC-relative
  branch). The values live in the thread's JITPointers (Dispatcher*),
  filled by InitThreadPointers.
- The DumpDispatcher option writes the generated code to a file; WinPad's
  tools/cache2macho --dispatcher turns it into the symbols
  fexaot_dispatcher_text and fexaot_dispatcher_meta, and
  tools/cache2macho/aotindex.py --dispatcher gives it an index entry (file
  id 0, config id ~0).
- With such an entry in the AOT index, the dispatcher is emitted into
  memory that is not executable (and has no RW alias). If the image's copy
  is byte for byte the same, every entry point moves to it ("[aot-dispatcher]
  static code from the AOT image"); otherwise the log says where they
  differ and FEX emits it again into the JIT pool and uses that.
- The AOT index parsing moves from ImageTracker into FEXCore/Core/AOTIndex.h,
  shared by both.

